10. XtaCache Investigation

The XtaCache service was configured as:

Service Name: XtaCache
Display Name: XtaCache
State: Running
Start Mode: Auto
Start Account: LocalSystem
Path:
C:\Windows\System32\XtaCache.exe

An automatically started SYSTEM service can provide a powerful persistence mechanism, so the executable was investigated further.

Digital Signature Validation

The executable was checked using PowerShell:

Get-AuthenticodeSignature "C:\Windows\System32\XtaCache.exe"
Result
Status: Valid
StatusMessage: Signature verified.
Signer: Microsoft Windows
Organization: Microsoft Corporation

This provided strong evidence that the executable was an authentic Microsoft-signed Windows component.

File Metadata
CompanyName: Microsoft Corporation
FileDescription: XTA Cache Service
FileVersion: 10.0.26100.1
ProductName: Microsoft Windows Operating System
ProductVersion: 10.0.26100.1
OriginalFilename: XtaCache.exe.mui

The metadata was consistent with a Microsoft Windows system component.

SHA-256 Hash
D66BDC10A4644DA89DE7CF87CE8B6377F73A3A3282D09A04C78D04588D4AF2BD

The hash was recorded to provide a unique cryptographic identifier for the examined file.

Assessment

🟢 Benign

The combination of:

C:\Windows\System32 location
Valid Microsoft Windows signature
Microsoft Corporation publisher
Windows OS product metadata
Identifiable XTA Cache Service description

strongly supports the conclusion that XtaCache.exe is a legitimate Windows component.

11. Security Event Log Correlation

Windows Security Event ID 4697 was queried to identify service installation events.

Command used:

Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{
    LogName='Security'
    Id=4697
} -MaxEvents 20
Result
No events were found that match the specified selection criteria.
Assessment

No Event ID 4697 events were identified in the available Security log.

Investigation Limitation

The absence of Event ID 4697 does not prove that no services were installed.

Event generation depends on appropriate Windows auditing configuration and available event-log retention.

Therefore, this result was treated as a telemetry limitation, rather than definitive proof that no service installation occurred.

