13. Final Assessment
🟢 No Malicious Windows Persistence Identified

The investigation did not identify evidence of malicious persistence through the Scheduled Task or Windows Service mechanisms examined.

Potentially unusual entries were investigated rather than automatically classified as malicious.

The XtaCache service was validated using:

File location
Service configuration
Digital signature
Microsoft signer information
File metadata
SHA-256 hashing

The virtualization-related services were also consistent with the known UTM/QEMU environment.

No suspicious executable paths, PowerShell persistence, command-shell persistence, scripts, or clearly malicious service configurations were identified.

14. Analyst Reasoning

This investigation demonstrated the importance of validating suspicious-looking artifacts before assigning a malicious classification.

A legitimate Windows executable such as:

rundll32.exe

can be abused by attackers, but the presence of the binary alone is not evidence of malicious activity.

Likewise, a service running as:

LocalSystem

has significant privileges but is common for legitimate Windows services.

The investigation therefore relied on contextual evidence including:

File location
Task/service configuration
Execution mechanism
Account context
Digital signatures
File metadata
SHA-256 hashing
Event log telemetry
Known virtualization environment
